## Course overview

Biology at Higher Level explores life from the molecular scale to the biosphere. Students build a deep understanding of the unifying themes of the IB Biology guide — **unity and diversity, form and function, interaction and interdependence, and continuity and change** — and apply them across every topic.

Across two years students cover cell biology, molecular biology, genetics, ecology, evolution, and human physiology, supported by extensive hands-on laboratory and fieldwork.

## What you will study

- **Cells** — cell structure, membrane transport, the origin of cells, and cell division.
- **Molecular biology** — water, carbohydrates, lipids, proteins, enzymes, and the chemistry of respiration and photosynthesis.
- **Genetics & evolution** — inheritance, gene expression, mutation, and the evidence for evolution by natural selection.
- **Ecology** — energy flow, nutrient cycling, climate change, and conservation of biodiversity.
- **Human physiology (HL)** — digestion, the cardiovascular and respiratory systems, defence against disease, and the nervous system.

## Assessment

| Component | Weighting | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Paper 1 | 36% | Multiple choice and data-based questions |
| Paper 2 | 44% | Short-answer and extended-response questions |
| Internal Assessment | 20% | An individual scientific investigation |

## The Internal Assessment

The IA is a single investigation of your own design, written up as a report of around 3,000 words. It is marked against five criteria — **research design, data analysis, conclusion, evaluation, and communication** — and rewards genuine scientific curiosity rather than a "correct" result.
